.TH CAMERA 3
.SH NAME
camera \- a camera's frames, raw and debayered
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #V /dev

.B /dev/camera/ctl
.B /dev/camera/modes
.B /dev/camera/frame
.B /dev/camera/raw
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The camera device serves one camera.
Its frames come from a
.IR source :
so far only
.BR test ,
a simulated sensor that makes what a Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3
(Sony IMX708) makes \(em raw 10-bit Bayer samples, red at the top left,
in the sensor's binned modes \(em with a test pattern in them.
A real camera's driver adds itself beside it as another source.
.IR osinit (8)
binds the device after
.BR /dev .
.PP
Reading
.B ctl
gives the camera's state, one setting a line:
.IP
.EX
source test
mode 640 480
fps 30
stream off
raw rggb10
frames 0
.EE
.PP
The
.B raw
line names what the
.B raw
file holds: the colour filter's two-by-two cell, read left to right and top
to bottom, and the bits in each sample.
.B frames
counts the frames the source has made since
.BR "stream on" .
The others are written as they are read, one a write:
.TF "\fLsource \fIname"
.TP
.BI source " name
Take frames from the named source, in the mode it starts in.
.TP
.BI mode " w h
Use one of the source's modes, as listed in
.BR modes .
If the mode's maximum frame rate is below
.BR fps ,
.B fps
is lowered to it.
.TP
.BI fps " n
Frames a second, from 1 to the mode's maximum.
.TP
.B "stream on\fR|\fPoff
Start or stop making frames.
.PD
.PP
.BR source ,
.B mode
and
.B fps
are refused while streaming.
.PP
.B Modes
lists what can be asked for, one mode a line:
.IP
.EX
.I "source width height maxfps rawformat
test 2304 1296 56 rggb10
test 1536 864 120 rggb10
test 640 480 60 rggb10
.EE
.PP
Opening
.B frame
waits for the next frame to be complete and takes it; reads return that
frame, and end of file at its end.
The next frame is the next open: a frame is never given out twice, and
a reader of any speed reads a whole one.
It is an uncompressed
.IR image (6)
file,
.BR r8g8b8 ,
which
.B Display.readimage
(see
.IR draw-display (2))
reads.
The source's samples are turned into it here, by bilinear interpolation.
.PP
.B Raw
is the same, as the source gave it: one sample a pixel, 16 bits,
little-endian, row by row, with no header.
Its width, height and Bayer order are
.BR ctl 's,
as
.BR /dev/audio 's
format is
.BR audioctl 's
(see
.IR audio (3)).
.PP
Opening either is refused unless the camera is streaming.
.PP
The test pattern is eight bars across the frame \(em white, yellow,
cyan, green, magenta, red, blue and black, each channel at full scale or
nothing \(em and a grey band, half scale and 16 rows high, that moves
down four rows a frame.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
; echo mode 640 480 > /dev/camera/ctl
; echo stream on > /dev/camera/ctl
; cat /dev/camera/frame > /tmp/shot.bit
; echo stream off > /dev/camera/ctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/port/devcam.c
.br
.B /os/port/campattern.c
.br
.B /os/port/camera.h
.br
.B /os/init/camtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR audio (3),
.IR bench (3),
.IR image (6),
.IR osinit (8)
.SH BUGS
Only the test source so far: no exposure, white balance or focus, and
no real camera.
A frame is debayered in the open that takes it, so a large mode's open
takes the processor's time for it.
.PP
An agent that can read
.B frame
or
.B raw
can see the room the machine is in: grant either only to an agent with
no way to send it anywhere, and never grant
.BR ctl .
